bin/
run/
*.jsonl
runs/

# Python's bytecode, left beside the test helpers that uv runs.
__pycache__/

# The test fixtures are rows too, and the *.jsonl rule above would swallow them.
# They are a handful of lines written by hand, with the figures they summarise to
# worked out on paper beside them, so they belong in the repository.
!internal/bench/testdata/**

# Sweep output is ignored above because a full pass is hundreds of megabytes
# across ~220 cells. Reference measurements cited by the README are different in
# kind and are kept, so every published figure stays recomputable from the rows
# it came from rather than being an assertion.
!docs/measurements/**

# ...but a sweep's raw rows are committed gzipped, so the plain form is re-ignored
# here. A full four-policy pass is 183 MB of .jsonl against 22 MB with every row
# kept, and the rows stay the system of record either way (ADR-0002) because
# .jsonl.gz is not what this pattern matches. Without the line, `git add` on a
# measurement directory stages the uncompressed copy and nothing objects. Rows
# already tracked in their plain form are unaffected — .gitignore does not reach a
# tracked file — and a small new one that is genuinely worth keeping uncompressed
# takes `git add -f`.
docs/measurements/**/*.jsonl

# A cell's own rows are not kept, in either form. Compaction folds every cell's
# rows into the sweep's requests.parquet, so the per-cell copies are 84 files and
# 8.8 MB restating what one 6.2 MB file already holds — and nothing reads them: a
# comparison is rebuilt from the cell records beside them (cells/*.json), which
# are kept. The router's own rows are a different measurement, not a duplicate of
# these, and are kept gzipped at the top of a measurement directory.
docs/measurements/**/cells/*.jsonl.gz
docs/measurements/2026-09-16-load-sweep-4policy
# Local editor and OS clutter, and the binary `go build ./cmd/preflight` leaves at the root.
# Anchored, so it cannot swallow cmd/preflight/.
.DS_Store
.vscode
/preflight
